WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.970 --> 00:00:05.705
- Hi, Mom.
- Hi, Cleo.

00:00:05.738 --> 00:00:07.306 align:start position:0%
Okay, we're ready.

00:00:07.339 --> 00:00:09.609
How did you decide

00:00:09.642 --> 00:00:11.511
whether or not you wanted
to have kids?

00:00:11.544 --> 00:00:15.281 align:start position:0%
It just started like
a natural flower blooming

00:00:15.314 --> 00:00:18.551
in my mind that,
"Okay, maybe children."

00:00:18.584 --> 00:00:21.354 line:0%
I grew up thinking
I would become a mom

00:00:21.387 --> 00:00:23.689 align:start position:0%
because I didn't know
that I could choose not to be.

00:00:23.722 --> 00:00:26.259 align:start position:0%
I am so glad
I don't have children.

00:00:26.292 --> 00:00:27.794 align:start position:0%
I knew I was always
gonna have kids.

00:00:27.827 --> 00:00:30.063 size:84% position:42%
I decided to start thinking

00:00:30.096 --> 00:00:32.331 align:start position:0%
seriously about
getting pregnant myself.

00:00:32.364 --> 00:00:34.801 line:0%
I've literally had people
upset at the fact

00:00:34.834 --> 00:00:36.502 line:0%
that I don't want
to have children.

00:00:36.535 --> 00:00:38.271 line:0%
But I'm like, "How does that
affect your life?"

00:00:38.304 --> 00:00:41.174 align:start position:0%
Especially, like,
factoring in everything

00:00:41.207 --> 00:00:43.342 align:start position:0% line:0%
that's happening
in the background,
including climate change,

00:00:43.375 --> 00:00:46.379
is this the kind of world
that I want to bring
my child into?

00:00:46.412 --> 00:00:49.615 align:start position:0% line:0%
This is what we wanted.
This was our goal,
and we're here.

00:00:49.648 --> 00:00:51.350
Whatever change you think
your life is gonna have

00:00:51.383 --> 00:00:54.454
in a significant way,
multiply it by, like, 99.

00:00:54.487 --> 00:00:57.523 align:start position:0%
Life? Blown up.
Completely gone.

00:00:57.556 --> 00:00:59.625
The vast majority
of Americans have kids,

00:00:59.658 --> 00:01:02.195
<i> but the number of births
 in the US</i>

00:01:02.228 --> 00:01:03.396
<i> has been slowly
 creeping downward</i>

00:01:03.429 --> 00:01:05.565
<i> over the past few decades.</i>

00:01:05.598 --> 00:01:08.134
<i> I think I want
 to become a parent,</i>

00:01:08.167 --> 00:01:10.369
<i> and I'm lucky to be able
 to make that decision.</i>

00:01:10.402 --> 00:01:11.804
<i> To be honest though,
 I still feel like</i>

00:01:11.837 --> 00:01:14.640
<i> there's so much
 that I don't know.</i>

00:01:14.673 --> 00:01:17.543
So my question is
how do I decide

00:01:17.576 --> 00:01:19.612
if I really want
to have kids?

00:01:21.313 --> 00:01:23.850
<i> ( babies laughing )</i>

00:01:23.883 --> 00:01:26.386 line:0%
<i> ( theme music playing )</i>

00:01:36.595 --> 00:01:39.398 line:0%
You know, it's really
hard to separate out

00:01:39.431 --> 00:01:41.400 line:0%
my desire to have kids

00:01:41.433 --> 00:01:44.337 line:0%
versus the expectation
to have kids.

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:45.571 line:0%
And if you don't believe
that there's still

00:01:45.604 --> 00:01:47.240 line:0%
an expectation
of having children,

00:01:47.273 --> 00:01:48.708
just turn on a television.

00:01:48.741 --> 00:01:51.744 align:start position:0%
- So, when are the kids coming?
- Ya gonna have a baby!

00:01:51.777 --> 00:01:53.579
- You gotta have one now.
- People like pregnant.

00:01:53.612 --> 00:01:54.747 align:start size:53% position:47%
Do you want to be
a mother or not?

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:56.415 align:start position:0%
Do you regret it,
not having children?

00:01:56.448 --> 00:01:57.817
There is the stigma
that if you don't have kids,

00:01:57.850 --> 00:01:59.452
then you're clearly
not as happy.

00:01:59.485 --> 00:02:02.421
A lot of us have real questions
about when and how

00:02:02.454 --> 00:02:04.423
and whether
to become parents.

00:02:04.456 --> 00:02:06.425
So, I'm gonna get some data,

00:02:06.458 --> 00:02:09.262
and why not start
with that big assumption,

00:02:09.295 --> 00:02:11.731
that kids make you happier?

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:17.770 line:0%
Since about the 1960s,
we've had pretty clear evidence

00:02:17.803 --> 00:02:22.241 line:0%
that being a parent
doesn't make you happier
than not having children.

00:02:22.274 --> 00:02:24.377
Out of the 20 countries
that we studied,

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:27.280
the United States
had the largest gap,

00:02:27.313 --> 00:02:31.284
where parents report
that they're significantly
less happy than non-parents.

00:02:31.317 --> 00:02:34.187
How do you ask survey questions
that get at this?

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:36.789
We're not asking people,
"Are you happy you had kids?"

00:02:36.822 --> 00:02:38.891
We're asking people,
"Are you happy?",

00:02:38.924 --> 00:02:42.495
and just asking them
to rate their own level
of happiness.

00:02:42.528 --> 00:02:44.697
 Cleo: It's not just
the United States.

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:48.334
We see the happiness gap
in all of these countries, too.

00:02:48.367 --> 00:02:50.636
Jennifer:
It's really a parental
happiness deficit.

00:02:50.669 --> 00:02:52.471
And it doesn't occur everywhere.

00:02:52.504 --> 00:02:53.906
There are a few nation states

00:02:53.939 --> 00:02:55.942
where parenthood
actually improves

00:02:55.975 --> 00:02:57.944
your average rated happiness,

00:02:57.977 --> 00:02:59.979
and some of those countries

00:03:00.012 --> 00:03:02.315
have extremely low
fertility rates.

00:03:02.348 --> 00:03:04.750
That's telling you that people
who do decide to have children

00:03:04.783 --> 00:03:10.690
are unusually attached to kids,
and want kids very badly.

00:03:10.723 --> 00:03:14.327
Now, we do have some
LGBT couples in the data,

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:16.963
but they differ in size
across all of these nations,

00:03:16.996 --> 00:03:18.864
and some nation states
did not even ask

00:03:18.897 --> 00:03:20.499
about the gender
of your partners.

00:03:20.532 --> 00:03:23.836
We're just beginning
to understand the dynamics

00:03:23.869 --> 00:03:27.607
and whether they're
similar or different from
heterosexual couples.

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:28.841
Does the happiness gap differ

00:03:28.874 --> 00:03:32.678
depending on wealth,
race, gender,

00:03:32.711 --> 00:03:35.414
or other demographics
within the United States?

00:03:35.447 --> 00:03:36.849
It really doesn't.

00:03:36.882 --> 00:03:39.986
We thought perhaps
that the gap would be larger

00:03:40.019 --> 00:03:42.822
for single parents, and it is.

00:03:42.855 --> 00:03:45.591
We thought perhaps the gap
would be larger for mothers

00:03:45.624 --> 00:03:47.693
than fathers, and it isn't.

00:03:47.726 --> 00:03:49.862
It grows when you take away
the partnership

00:03:49.895 --> 00:03:51.731
and the stable employment,

00:03:51.764 --> 00:03:52.999
but once you control
for those factors,

00:03:53.032 --> 00:03:55.534
it's fairly stable
across race.

00:03:55.567 --> 00:03:58.771
How do parents react though,
when you tell them
about this data?

00:03:58.804 --> 00:04:01.040
Oh, they hate it.
Some of them don't believe it.

00:04:01.073 --> 00:04:03.409
I have to show them the data

00:04:03.442 --> 00:04:06.012
from one survey after another
after another.

00:04:06.045 --> 00:04:09.515
But if we ask parents,
"Have your children
made you happy?",

00:04:09.548 --> 00:04:11.017
they would all say yes.

00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:12.385
I would say yes.

00:04:12.418 --> 00:04:14.287
Everybody I know
would say yes.

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:17.390
But what their children
have brought into their lives

00:04:17.423 --> 00:04:22.728
in many ways is anxiety, stress,
and financial trouble

00:04:22.761 --> 00:04:25.265
that they would
not have experienced.

00:04:30.602 --> 00:04:33.739 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Did you know about
  the happiness gap?
- I did.

00:04:33.772 --> 00:04:35.841 align:start position:0%
What's your take
on how seriously
we should take this data?

00:04:35.874 --> 00:04:37.910 align:start size:81% position:19% line:0%
I might be a little biased
because I've always

00:04:37.943 --> 00:04:40.913 align:start size:66% position:34% line:0%
been quite ambivalent
about the idea
of having children,

00:04:40.946 --> 00:04:43.616 align:start size:69% position:31% line:0%
but I think it's worth
taking very seriously.

00:04:43.649 --> 00:04:45.885
There is a huge cultural myth

00:04:45.918 --> 00:04:49.588 align:start size:75% position:25%
that kids are going
to bring nothing but joy
to your life,

00:04:49.621 --> 00:04:51.390 size:78% position:61%
and I think that leads
a lot of parents going in

00:04:51.423 --> 00:04:53.592 align:start size:69% position:31%
with maybe
the wrong expectation.

00:04:53.625 --> 00:04:56.429 align:start position:0%
I gotta say,
I look at this data,

00:04:56.462 --> 00:05:01.600 align:start position:0%
and I think to myself,
"Yikes!"

00:05:01.633 --> 00:05:03.869 align:start position:0%
And then I think,
"I probably still want kids."

00:05:03.902 --> 00:05:06.772 align:start size:91% position:9%
- It's such a weird, um...
- Why?

00:05:06.805 --> 00:05:08.541 align:start position:0%
I don't know.

00:05:08.574 --> 00:05:09.975 align:start size:69% position:31%
If you think about it,

00:05:10.008 --> 00:05:13.045 align:start size:84% position:16%
our species has been around
for 300,000 years.

00:05:13.078 --> 00:05:18.417 align:start size:72% position:28%
The ability to control
female reproduction
is about 60 years old.

00:05:18.450 --> 00:05:22.455 align:start size:72% position:28%
Cultural and economic
liberation for women
is still in progress.

00:05:22.488 --> 00:05:26.092 align:start size:72% position:28%
We are among
the first generations
of life on Earth,

00:05:26.125 --> 00:05:28.961 align:start size:91% position:9%
which is the only life
that we know in the universe,

00:05:28.994 --> 00:05:31.097 align:start size:63% position:38%
that can choose
not to reproduce.

00:05:31.130 --> 00:05:33.899 align:start size:63% position:38%
To me, that's
incredibly exciting.

00:05:33.932 --> 00:05:37.903 align:start size:66% position:34%
But the act of making
that decision final?

00:05:37.936 --> 00:05:39.705
I still wish I had more time.

00:05:39.738 --> 00:05:43.476 align:start position:0%
That's another part of this
that I feel really
anxious about.

00:05:43.509 --> 00:05:46.045 align:start size:63% position:38%
Our biology does not
keep up with our
social progress.

00:05:46.078 --> 00:05:47.980 align:start size:84% position:16%
And that makes me so angry.
Can I tell you?

00:05:48.013 --> 00:05:52.151 align:start size:81% position:19%
Like, the cosmic injustice
of this, that, like,

00:05:52.184 --> 00:05:54.120 align:start size:72% position:28%
just as you're starting
to feel comfortable
in your own skin,

00:05:54.153 --> 00:05:56.822 align:start size:63% position:38%
maybe you've started
to build something,

00:05:56.855 --> 00:05:58.724 align:start size:81% position:19%
that's when you're slapped
with this decision that,

00:05:58.757 --> 00:06:01.827 size:84% position:58%
"Hey, you need to turn your
life upside down right now

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:03.129 size:88% position:56%
or you can't have a family."

00:06:03.162 --> 00:06:06.132 align:start size:75% position:25%
I mean,
in the biological sense.

00:06:06.165 --> 00:06:09.468 align:start size:81% position:19%
Maybe people with kids
are this, like, crazy club

00:06:09.501 --> 00:06:12.138 size:84% position:58%
of, like, unhappy people
that want you to join them.

00:06:12.171 --> 00:06:15.674
But that sort of discounts
all the other parts.

00:06:15.707 --> 00:06:19.412 align:start size:84% position:16%
I don't think it captures
just, like, the fulfillment
that you receive.

00:06:19.445 --> 00:06:21.414 align:start size:59% position:41%
I mean, I just love
every moment.

00:06:21.447 --> 00:06:24.116 align:start size:91% position:9%
Like, she opens up
her hands to hold her bottle?
Like, I'm...

00:06:24.149 --> 00:06:27.019
Fabiola:
Having a child-- beautiful
thing, beautiful feat.

00:06:27.052 --> 00:06:30.523 line:0%
But I feel like
there's just so much stress
surrounding it,

00:06:30.556 --> 00:06:31.757 line:0%
especially in this country,

00:06:31.790 --> 00:06:33.192 align:start position:0%
and I think that's
the main issue,

00:06:33.225 --> 00:06:34.960 align:start position:0%
of just, like,
I like the way things
are right now.

00:06:34.993 --> 00:06:39.165
But with where things are now,
I need to make a hard decision.

00:06:39.198 --> 00:06:41.000 align:start position:0%
I am a single mother
of four daughters.

00:06:41.033 --> 00:06:45.671
There isn't anyone else coming
to share that load with you.

00:06:45.704 --> 00:06:47.706 align:start position:0%
But these are the girls,
these are my girls,

00:06:47.739 --> 00:06:50.009
where if they saw me tear up,

00:06:50.042 --> 00:06:53.546 align:start position:0%
they would go fix up a sandwich,
and they'd be like,

00:06:53.579 --> 00:06:54.914 align:start position:0%
"Hey, do you want
to eat something?"

00:06:54.947 --> 00:06:56.882 align:start position:0%
I was raised a girl.

00:06:56.915 --> 00:06:59.885 align:start position:0%
The world taught me
that my needs mattered less

00:06:59.918 --> 00:07:02.721 align:start position:0%
than literally
everyone around me.

00:07:02.754 --> 00:07:04.056
And so I think
actually the best thing

00:07:04.089 --> 00:07:06.625
I can do for my family
is to be happy.

00:07:06.658 --> 00:07:08.828
Sometimes that means
when you get in the car
and they're like,

00:07:08.861 --> 00:07:10.763
"Oh, can we listen
to my music?"

00:07:10.796 --> 00:07:12.965
You say, "No, we're gonna
listen to my podcast."

00:07:12.998 --> 00:07:17.236
And they might whine,
but it makes me way happier
and way less resentful

00:07:17.269 --> 00:07:21.106
than I'm listening
to "Row Your Boat" for
the 80th time today.

00:07:21.139 --> 00:07:22.508
Like...

00:07:27.846 --> 00:07:31.050
Woman:
Scooch down towards me.

00:07:31.083 --> 00:07:34.086
Ready? All right.
A lot of pressure here.

00:07:34.119 --> 00:07:36.589
This is a view of your uterus.

00:07:36.622 --> 00:07:39.258 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Cleo: W-where?
- This whole thing here.

00:07:39.291 --> 00:07:42.127 line:0%
Looks kind of long,
like an eggplant or a banana.

00:07:42.160 --> 00:07:44.096 align:start position:0%
For a woman who's
never been pregnant,

00:07:44.129 --> 00:07:46.866 align:start position:0%
it's about the size
of a bottle of nail polish.

00:07:46.899 --> 00:07:49.602 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Huh.
- What we do is just
  take a look here,

00:07:49.635 --> 00:07:51.904
make sure everything
looks pretty normal,

00:07:51.937 --> 00:07:55.508
which it does. Okay.

00:07:55.541 --> 00:07:57.510 align:start size:56% position:44%
We have plenty
of great pictures.

00:07:57.543 --> 00:08:00.112 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Do I get to keep it,
  like from a party?
- Like a souvenir.

00:08:00.145 --> 00:08:02.815 align:start position:0%
Wild.

00:08:02.848 --> 00:08:04.750 align:start position:0%
What do you feel like
are the biggest misconceptions

00:08:04.783 --> 00:08:07.853 align:start position:0%
that you hear
about the relationship
between fertility and age?

00:08:07.886 --> 00:08:10.523 align:start size:81% position:19%
That you have
all the time in the world.

00:08:10.556 --> 00:08:12.958 align:start size:72% position:28%
With time,
fertility does go down.

00:08:12.991 --> 00:08:15.160
Cleo:
<i> One of the best ways
 to see this</i>

00:08:15.193 --> 00:08:16.795
<i> is just the chance
 a person has</i>

00:08:16.828 --> 00:08:18.564
<i> of getting pregnant
 each month.</i>

00:08:18.597 --> 00:08:21.133
<i> Doctors call that
 fecundability.</i>

00:08:21.166 --> 00:08:24.270 align:start size:63% position:38%
Your mid to late-20s
is actually when
you're most fertile.

00:08:24.303 --> 00:08:26.906 align:start size:66% position:34%
It's about 15 to 25%.

00:08:26.939 --> 00:08:32.778 align:start size:63% position:38%
That can decrease
in the mid-30s
to about 10 to 15%.

00:08:32.811 --> 00:08:36.282 align:start size:75% position:25%
And unfortunately,
over about age 42 to 44,

00:08:36.315 --> 00:08:38.984 align:start size:72% position:28%
it can be less than 2%.

00:08:39.017 --> 00:08:41.654
Cleo:
<i> One reason for this decline
 is the number of eggs</i>

00:08:41.687 --> 00:08:44.823
<i> that you have goes down
 as you age-- egg quantity.</i>

00:08:44.856 --> 00:08:47.293
<i> But fertility is also
 about egg quality,</i>

00:08:47.326 --> 00:08:49.895
<i> or how many
 of those remaining eggs</i>

00:08:49.928 --> 00:08:51.830
<i> are chromosomally normal.</i>

00:08:51.863 --> 00:08:54.833 align:start size:63% position:38%
The vast majority
of early miscarriage

00:08:54.866 --> 00:08:56.769 align:start size:69% position:31%
is actually due
to chromosomal errors.

00:08:56.802 --> 00:09:00.673
A lot of people tend to think
of children with Down Syndrome

00:09:00.706 --> 00:09:03.876 align:start size:84% position:16%
or Turner Syndrome,
but those are the outliers.

00:09:03.909 --> 00:09:06.312 align:start position:0%
Got it.
That makes a lot of sense.

00:09:06.345 --> 00:09:09.148 size:75% position:38%
Okay, so, we're gonna do
a little visualization

00:09:09.181 --> 00:09:12.851 align:start position:0%
so that you can show us
for a person who can
bear children,

00:09:12.884 --> 00:09:15.821 align:start position:0%
what percentage of their eggs
are chromosomally normal

00:09:15.854 --> 00:09:18.057 align:start position:0%
on average
as they get older?

00:09:18.090 --> 00:09:21.994
So, blue is gonna be
genetically normal eggs

00:09:22.027 --> 00:09:24.697
and yellow's gonna be
genetically abnormal.

00:09:24.730 --> 00:09:25.731 align:start size:16% position:84%
Okay.

00:09:29.067 --> 00:09:32.237 align:start position:0% line:0%
Okay, so this is 28.
This is my age.

00:09:32.270 --> 00:09:36.175 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
You have a really high chance
of making a pregnancy

00:09:36.208 --> 00:09:39.712 align:start size:75% position:25%
or embryos with a normal
number of chromosomes.

00:09:39.745 --> 00:09:42.715 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
But as you see,
it's never going to be zero.

00:09:42.748 --> 00:09:46.051 align:start size:75% position:25% line:0%
So, as you age to 33,
there's a higher chance,

00:09:46.084 --> 00:09:49.221 align:start size:66% position:34% line:0%
maybe about 30 to 31%
at this point.

00:09:49.254 --> 00:09:54.226 align:start size:75% position:25% line:0%
But around age 37 to 38,
the chances of having

00:09:54.259 --> 00:09:59.064 size:88% position:56% line:0%
a pregnancy with chromosomal
abnormalities is almost 50%.

00:09:59.097 --> 00:10:02.167 align:start size:75% position:25% line:0%
That's a number that's
very shocking to people,

00:10:02.200 --> 00:10:05.938 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
but, you know,
as we advance in age over 42,

00:10:05.971 --> 00:10:08.207 size:84% position:58% line:0%
the chance gets quite high.

00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:11.744 align:start size:72% position:28% line:0%
It's now over 80%,
and so this contributes

00:10:11.777 --> 00:10:13.679 align:start size:53% position:47% line:0%
to why women
in that age group

00:10:13.712 --> 00:10:15.914 align:start size:69% position:31% line:0%
may have a harder time
getting pregnant

00:10:15.947 --> 00:10:18.017 align:start size:72% position:28%
and may, unfortunately,
experience miscarriage

00:10:18.050 --> 00:10:20.686 align:start size:81% position:19%
much higher than they were
when they were younger.

00:10:20.719 --> 00:10:25.424 align:start size:72% position:28%
Sometimes we're able
to make generalizations
based on your age,

00:10:25.457 --> 00:10:27.393 align:start size:50% position:50%
but the only way
to know for sure

00:10:27.426 --> 00:10:30.829 align:start size:69% position:31%
is an evaluation
of your own individual
reproductive health.

00:10:30.862 --> 00:10:33.399 align:start position:0%
What do you see
as the most important
disparities to talk about

00:10:33.432 --> 00:10:36.168 align:start position:0%
with respect to access
to fertility care?

00:10:36.201 --> 00:10:38.971 align:start size:84% position:16%
Cost is probably
one of the biggest factors.

00:10:39.004 --> 00:10:43.842
One cycle of IVF can typically
be about $15 to $20,000.

00:10:43.875 --> 00:10:45.944
Cleo:
<i> IVF isn't the only option,</i>

00:10:45.977 --> 00:10:48.781
<i> but all of the options
 aren't cheap,</i>

00:10:48.814 --> 00:10:51.784
<i> and often not covered
 by insurance.</i>

00:10:51.817 --> 00:10:55.187 align:start size:94% position:6%
Something else is looking
at the differences in outcomes

00:10:55.220 --> 00:10:57.389 size:88% position:56%
based on race and ethnicity.

00:10:57.422 --> 00:10:59.425 align:start size:75% position:25%
There are studies
that show women of color

00:10:59.458 --> 00:11:03.429 size:88% position:56%
are referred to infertility
specialists less frequently,

00:11:03.462 --> 00:11:05.431 align:start size:84% position:16%
and that may play
into why they present later

00:11:05.464 --> 00:11:09.802 align:start size:72% position:28%
for care or with longer
periods of infertility.

00:11:09.835 --> 00:11:13.305 align:start position:0%
What can we do
to reduce those disparities?

00:11:13.338 --> 00:11:16.008 align:start size:72% position:28%
I think the first thing
we have to do
is acknowledge it.

00:11:16.041 --> 00:11:18.310 size:84% position:58%
We all have implicit biases

00:11:18.343 --> 00:11:20.813 align:start size:75% position:25%
that govern
how we conduct ourselves

00:11:20.846 --> 00:11:23.449 align:start size:78% position:22%
and how we react
to different people
and different situations.

00:11:23.482 --> 00:11:25.984 align:start size:81% position:19%
The patients that are
visiting fertility clinics

00:11:26.017 --> 00:11:29.822 align:start size:91% position:9%
tend to be white, female,
college-educated, and wealthy

00:11:29.855 --> 00:11:32.191 align:start size:69% position:31%
because that's who can
afford the treatment.

00:11:32.224 --> 00:11:33.826 align:start size:66% position:34%
And so one
of the biggest things

00:11:33.859 --> 00:11:35.494 size:84% position:58%
I personally would wish for

00:11:35.527 --> 00:11:38.997 align:start size:75% position:25%
is infertility
evaluation and treatment

00:11:39.030 --> 00:11:42.000 align:start size:72% position:28%
to be covered by health
insurance universally.

00:11:42.033 --> 00:11:44.336 align:start position:0%
We found out about
a fertility benefit.

00:11:44.369 --> 00:11:47.005 align:start position:0%
We met those requirements
to be able to enroll

00:11:47.038 --> 00:11:49.308 align:start position:0%
and we got started
with our IVF process,

00:11:49.341 --> 00:11:52.878 align:start position:0%
and here we are with this
little one today.

00:11:52.911 --> 00:11:57.382 align:start position:0%
I'm incredibly privileged
in that it cost me literally
$0 to get pregnant

00:11:57.415 --> 00:12:00.185 align:start position:0%
because of the particular
biological realities

00:12:00.218 --> 00:12:02.855
of my relationship.

00:12:02.888 --> 00:12:05.357
We had sperm, egg, uterus,
between the two of us.

00:12:05.390 --> 00:12:07.192
But most transgender people

00:12:07.225 --> 00:12:10.028
need some help
with some part of that.

00:12:10.061 --> 00:12:14.032
As I got older,
what I learned was that
as a black woman,

00:12:14.065 --> 00:12:18.203
I was at even more risk
for complications

00:12:18.236 --> 00:12:20.472
and death after childbirth.

00:12:20.505 --> 00:12:23.876
And it didn't instill
any confidence in me.

00:12:23.909 --> 00:12:26.378
I recently helped my sister
inject herself.

00:12:26.411 --> 00:12:27.846
She's freezing her eggs.

00:12:27.879 --> 00:12:29.982 align:start position:0%
That was just
a very powerful moment.

00:12:30.015 --> 00:12:33.018 align:start position:0%
I knew I was gonna have kids.
That was never an issue.

00:12:33.051 --> 00:12:35.521
The bigger thing was
what would you do strategically

00:12:35.554 --> 00:12:38.891
in order to make sure
you could maximize your career,

00:12:38.924 --> 00:12:40.126
which was important for me.

00:12:44.095 --> 00:12:47.065 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
- Good morning.
- Good morning! Welcome.

00:12:47.098 --> 00:12:48.901 align:start position:0% line:0%
What are we--
what are we doing here?

00:12:48.934 --> 00:12:50.903 align:start size:78% position:22% line:0%
So, there's a lot of data
that shows

00:12:50.936 --> 00:12:52.538 align:start size:78% position:22% line:0%
that the financial impact
of having a child

00:12:52.571 --> 00:12:54.540 align:start size:59% position:41% line:0%
isn't borne equally
by men and women.

00:12:54.573 --> 00:12:56.909 align:start size:78% position:22%
- So we're gonna
  visualize that.
- Okay.

00:12:56.942 --> 00:12:58.544 align:start size:75% position:25%
- Ready?
- I-I think so.

00:13:02.380 --> 00:13:05.050 align:start size:94% position:6%
- I feel very cool.
- We're like cave explorers.

00:13:07.485 --> 00:13:10.088 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Let's do it!
- ( Christophe laughs )

00:13:10.121 --> 00:13:12.991 align:start position:0% line:0%
This looks so tall.

00:13:13.024 --> 00:13:15.561
Cleo:<i> A 2017 report
 from the U.S. Census Bureau</i>

00:13:15.594 --> 00:13:18.564
<i> shows the impact
 of having a child on men
 and women's earnings</i>

00:13:18.597 --> 00:13:20.933
<i> among married
 heterosexual couples.</i>

00:13:20.966 --> 00:13:24.169 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Ready, Christophe?
- I'm scared.

00:13:24.202 --> 00:13:26.238
<i> The key chart
 looks something like this.</i>

00:13:26.271 --> 00:13:27.940
<i> The male and female spouse</i>

00:13:27.973 --> 00:13:29.308
<i> both start out at zero</i>

00:13:29.341 --> 00:13:31.276
<i> the year the baby is born.</i>

00:13:31.309 --> 00:13:32.945
<i> It's a way to control
 for differences</i>

00:13:32.978 --> 00:13:34.847
<i> between men
 and women's earnings</i>

00:13:34.880 --> 00:13:37.316
<i> so that we see the effects
 of having kids more easily.</i>

00:13:38.984 --> 00:13:40.586
<i> In the first year
 after having a child,</i>

00:13:40.619 --> 00:13:43.589
<i> the mother's earnings
 take a big hit.</i>

00:13:43.622 --> 00:13:46.124
<i> The father's? Not so much.</i>

00:13:46.157 --> 00:13:47.960 align:start position:0%
So, Christophe,
this is year one.

00:13:47.993 --> 00:13:51.129 align:start size:78% position:22%
- Jeez. Huge hit.
- Yeah.

00:13:51.162 --> 00:13:54.233
<i> The female spouse's earnings
 don't actually recover</i>

00:13:54.266 --> 00:13:57.469
<i> until the child
 is around nine years old.</i>

00:13:57.502 --> 00:13:59.605 align:start size:47% position:53%
So, essentially
at year nine,

00:13:59.638 --> 00:14:02.441 align:start size:81% position:19%
the mom's salary
has reset to where it was.

00:14:02.474 --> 00:14:03.642 align:start position:0%
Yeah.

00:14:03.675 --> 00:14:05.544 align:start size:81% position:19%
- All right, ready?
- Yeah.

00:14:05.577 --> 00:14:08.013 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Year 18. Go on up.
- 18!

00:14:09.447 --> 00:14:11.283 align:start position:0%
Look at me climb
the career ladder.

00:14:11.316 --> 00:14:15.087
<i>Though her earnings do increase
 as the child gets older,</i>

00:14:15.120 --> 00:14:17.990
<i>the gap between the two genders
 is still there</i>

00:14:18.023 --> 00:14:20.993
<i> by the time that kid
 is officially an adult.</i>

00:14:21.026 --> 00:14:25.163
<i> One huge factor here
 is unequal childcare
 responsibilities.</i>

00:14:25.196 --> 00:14:27.900
<i> According to
 the American Time Use Survey,</i>

00:14:27.933 --> 00:14:31.169
<i> on an average day,
 women spent more than double
 the amount of time</i>

00:14:31.202 --> 00:14:33.972
<i> providing physical care
 to children than men.</i>

00:14:34.005 --> 00:14:36.008 align:start size:66% position:34%
Yeah, I mean, so much
of this makes sense,

00:14:36.041 --> 00:14:38.610 align:start size:50% position:50%
thinking about
my mom's career.

00:14:38.643 --> 00:14:41.413 size:84% position:58%
You know, she took off work
until I was six or seven.

00:14:41.446 --> 00:14:43.081 align:start size:75% position:25%
It was great that
she was able to do that,

00:14:43.114 --> 00:14:45.150 align:start size:78% position:22%
but it's a hard trade-off
to have to have made.

00:14:45.183 --> 00:14:47.085
Yeah.

00:14:47.118 --> 00:14:48.353
Christophe, one more thing.

00:14:48.386 --> 00:14:52.357 align:start size:78% position:22%
- Yeah? Oh, okay.
- Look down.

00:14:52.390 --> 00:14:54.359 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Okay, now we're done.
- Whoo!

00:14:54.392 --> 00:14:58.063 align:start size:72% position:28%
- There it is!
- Whee!

00:15:05.003 --> 00:15:06.571 align:start size:75% position:25%
- Whew!
- How was that?

00:15:06.604 --> 00:15:09.574 align:start size:56% position:44%
You know, managed.
I'm a little sore.

00:15:09.607 --> 00:15:12.945 align:start size:72% position:28%
I feel like we could've
printed that chart out,
but it's fine.

00:15:12.978 --> 00:15:17.382 align:start position:0%
I really wanted to know
if there was, like, a good
or a bad time to have kids.

00:15:17.415 --> 00:15:18.550
- Like, what's the ideal?
- I really wanted someone

00:15:18.583 --> 00:15:20.986 align:start position:0%
to time it perfectly for me,
you know?

00:15:21.019 --> 00:15:22.988 align:start position:0%
What's interesting
is the data shows
that for couples

00:15:23.021 --> 00:15:27.392 align:start position:0%
who have children
under 25 or over 35,

00:15:27.425 --> 00:15:29.394 align:start position:0%
the female spouse
takes an earnings hit

00:15:29.427 --> 00:15:32.064 align:start position:0%
but actually does catch
back up to the male partner.

00:15:32.097 --> 00:15:35.467 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Interesting.
- But for the couples that
  have kids between 25 and 35,

00:15:35.500 --> 00:15:37.703
<i> the female partner
 never catches up.</i>

00:15:37.736 --> 00:15:40.172
It seems to sort of show that
there is this core window,

00:15:40.205 --> 00:15:43.075 align:start size:72% position:28%
25 to 35, where you are
starting your career,

00:15:43.108 --> 00:15:45.711 align:start size:69% position:31%
setting yourself up
for a successful life,

00:15:45.744 --> 00:15:48.213 align:start size:75% position:25%
and that's a hard window
to have a kid.

00:15:48.246 --> 00:15:50.082 align:start position:0%
The thing about
that data though

00:15:50.115 --> 00:15:53.085 align:start position:0%
is that it's so wrapped up in
socioeconomics and education....

00:15:53.118 --> 00:15:55.420 align:start size:91% position:9%
- Yeah.
- ...that it's really hard
  to tell what it means.

00:15:55.453 --> 00:15:58.056 align:start size:91% position:9%
- Yeah.
- Over the course
  of the last couple days,

00:15:58.089 --> 00:15:59.725 align:start position:0%
I've learned that
the fertility data tells you

00:15:59.758 --> 00:16:02.728 align:start position:0%
that probably
the safer choice to have kids
on the earlier side.

00:16:02.761 --> 00:16:05.197 align:start position:0%
And the career data
implies you might

00:16:05.230 --> 00:16:07.199 align:start position:0%
take less of a hit
if you have kids later,

00:16:07.232 --> 00:16:10.002
which is a little frustrating.

00:16:10.035 --> 00:16:11.503 align:start size:84% position:16%
This question of
should you have kids or not

00:16:11.536 --> 00:16:14.106 align:start size:63% position:38%
is such a completely
different question

00:16:14.139 --> 00:16:17.376 align:start size:69% position:31%
professionally for men
than it is for women.

00:16:17.409 --> 00:16:20.612
You were prioritizing
being at home

00:16:20.645 --> 00:16:23.615 size:88% position:44%
and changed jobs to do that.

00:16:23.648 --> 00:16:25.617
- That's true.
- Why was it you

00:16:25.650 --> 00:16:29.021
who made that choice
and not Dad?

00:16:29.054 --> 00:16:34.126 align:start size:66% position:34%
Well, it wasn't
completely voluntary.

00:16:34.159 --> 00:16:37.295 align:start size:75% position:25%
You know, when I went
back to work after Zoey,

00:16:37.328 --> 00:16:40.499 align:start size:66% position:34%
I basically was told,
"Okay, you're on
the mommy track now."

00:16:40.532 --> 00:16:42.467
I was young and very ambitious

00:16:42.500 --> 00:16:44.636
and I just thought
I could do everything

00:16:44.669 --> 00:16:46.772
no matter what the environment,

00:16:46.805 --> 00:16:49.274
and that turned out
not to be true.

00:16:49.307 --> 00:16:51.443 size:81% position:41%
I'm really afraid of that.

00:16:51.476 --> 00:16:54.312 align:start size:88% position:13%
Yeah, well, you can suss out
your environment a bit.

00:16:54.345 --> 00:16:56.548
There were no women
who were associates

00:16:56.581 --> 00:16:58.683
who had babies who
had gone on to make partner.

00:16:58.716 --> 00:17:00.485 align:start size:56% position:44%
I should have
learned from that.

00:17:00.518 --> 00:17:03.055 align:start position:0%
I don't think that's fair
to put on yourself.

00:17:03.088 --> 00:17:07.759
It really frustrates me
that we position this
as an individual choice.

00:17:07.792 --> 00:17:11.596 align:start position:0%
Like, the other option
is your law firm could
have supported you more.

00:17:11.629 --> 00:17:14.533
Yes, um, and they were not
prepared to do that.

00:17:20.205 --> 00:17:23.475 align:start position:0% line:0%
You know, nobody that
I've spoken to ever expected
parenting to be easy.

00:17:23.508 --> 00:17:25.277 size:78% position:39%
Like, I definitely don't.

00:17:25.310 --> 00:17:27.345
But I keep thinking
about this chart

00:17:27.378 --> 00:17:29.214
and where
the United States falls,

00:17:29.247 --> 00:17:32.117 size:84% position:42%
and I can't help but wonder
if it has to be this hard.

00:17:32.150 --> 00:17:34.686 align:start position:0%
So, my last question
is just what could we do

00:17:34.719 --> 00:17:37.322 align:start position:0%
to make it easier
to be a parent
in the United States?

00:17:37.355 --> 00:17:40.559 align:start position:0%
And one thing that
we could do is make
childcare more affordable.

00:17:40.592 --> 00:17:43.395
There's a new report
that just came out that shows

00:17:43.428 --> 00:17:45.864
as a percentage
of women's full-time earnings

00:17:45.897 --> 00:17:49.801
how much do women have
to pay for childcare
in each of these countries?

00:17:49.834 --> 00:17:51.503
So, each stack of coins
represents a woman's

00:17:51.536 --> 00:17:54.539
median full-time earnings
in each place.

00:17:54.572 --> 00:17:56.842
And we're gonna take out coins
to represent

00:17:56.875 --> 00:17:58.376
the amount of her earnings

00:17:58.409 --> 00:18:00.812
that she's gonna spend
on childcare.

00:18:00.845 --> 00:18:02.581
All right, so I'm not
gonna touch any of these.

00:18:02.614 --> 00:18:04.549
But I'm gonna take
one out for Germany.

00:18:04.582 --> 00:18:07.419 align:start size:88% position:13%
So, you removed for Germany,
but not for Chile,

00:18:07.452 --> 00:18:09.688
the Czech Republic,
or Italy.

00:18:09.721 --> 00:18:13.391 align:start position:0%
Yeah, for the most part,
governments in these countries
provide a lot of support.

00:18:13.424 --> 00:18:17.329 align:start size:91% position:9%
- Wow.
- So now we've got
  all of these countries.

00:18:17.362 --> 00:18:19.397
Let's see what
that looks like.

00:18:19.430 --> 00:18:22.868 align:start size:50% position:50%
Five, Korea,
six, Iceland,
seven, Portugal.

00:18:22.901 --> 00:18:24.803
By the way, this is,
like, $500 worth of coins.

00:18:24.836 --> 00:18:26.305 align:start position:0%
Yeah. ( laughs )

00:18:37.215 --> 00:18:39.751 align:start size:84% position:16%
So, it looks like
for all of these countries,

00:18:39.784 --> 00:18:43.555
they are here spending
a quarter of their earnings.

00:18:43.588 --> 00:18:47.692 align:start size:88% position:13%
But that is still
a significant chunk of money
to be spending on childcare.

00:18:47.725 --> 00:18:50.462
Right? That's a huge chunk,
even in the middle
of this chart.

00:18:50.495 --> 00:18:52.597
Finland is already
getting into territory

00:18:52.630 --> 00:18:54.699
that looks very different
from Germany,

00:18:54.732 --> 00:18:56.401 align:start size:63% position:38%
so I'm scared
to see what happens.

00:18:56.434 --> 00:18:57.836
So, the United States
is here,

00:18:57.869 --> 00:18:59.905 align:start position:0%
among a small group of
countries where women spend

00:18:59.938 --> 00:19:02.440 align:start position:0%
more than a third
of their full-time
earnings on childcare.

00:19:02.473 --> 00:19:05.343 align:start size:59% position:41%
That is very scary.

00:19:05.376 --> 00:19:08.413 align:start size:88% position:13%
- So, this is Australia.
- Whoa.

00:19:08.446 --> 00:19:10.315
Let's get rid of all of that.

00:19:10.348 --> 00:19:12.851 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Should I do Canada?
- Yeah, 34.

00:19:17.655 --> 00:19:20.926 align:start size:78% position:22%
- Boop.
- So there it is.

00:19:20.959 --> 00:19:22.794 align:start size:63% position:38%
I'm trying to get
a sense of why

00:19:22.827 --> 00:19:25.630
there's so much disparity
across the board.

00:19:25.663 --> 00:19:27.465
On this side,
from Germany to Italy,

00:19:27.498 --> 00:19:30.835
what is it that countries
on this end are not doing?

00:19:30.868 --> 00:19:33.772
Cleo: Denmark, for example,
mandates that parents

00:19:33.805 --> 00:19:36.908 align:start position:0%
can't be charged more
than 25% of the operating
costs of childcare.

00:19:36.941 --> 00:19:39.277 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Wow. Okay.
- And the government
  covers the rest.

00:19:39.310 --> 00:19:41.313
In Korea, they were
very concerned

00:19:41.346 --> 00:19:43.381
about falling birth rates,

00:19:43.414 --> 00:19:47.686 align:start position:0%
and so they spend
a lot of money on
supporting parents.

00:19:47.719 --> 00:19:49.955 align:start position:0%
And one of the things
that they do is they
offer cash benefits.

00:19:49.988 --> 00:19:53.658 align:start size:94% position:6%
Wow, so it sounds like
parents there are incentivized
to actually have kids.

00:19:53.691 --> 00:19:56.828 align:start position:0%
Yeah. Given that finances
are a really big part

00:19:56.861 --> 00:19:58.830 align:start position:0%
of the decision to have kids
for a lot of people,

00:19:58.863 --> 00:20:01.967
it does seem like that decision
would be less burdensome

00:20:02.000 --> 00:20:04.369
in a lot of these
other countries

00:20:04.402 --> 00:20:05.737 align:start position:0%
than it would be
for a lot of parents
in the United States.

00:20:05.770 --> 00:20:07.305 align:start size:16% position:84%
Yeah.

00:20:07.338 --> 00:20:08.540
I've gone through
this whole journey

00:20:08.573 --> 00:20:11.343
learning about
parental happiness data

00:20:11.376 --> 00:20:13.678
and fertility data
and earnings data,

00:20:13.711 --> 00:20:19.017 align:start position:0%
and having it help me
as I'm making this decision.

00:20:19.050 --> 00:20:22.354
The truth is, I'm not
gonna be making this decision

00:20:22.387 --> 00:20:24.990 align:start position:0%
based on statistics.

00:20:25.023 --> 00:20:26.858 align:start position:0%
I'm gonna be making
this decision based on

00:20:26.891 --> 00:20:29.861
<i> my own feelings
 and my own family</i>

00:20:29.894 --> 00:20:32.364
<i> and my own
 potential family.</i>

00:20:32.397 --> 00:20:36.401
<i> And one of the things that
 I've been thinking a lot about</i>

00:20:36.434 --> 00:20:40.538
<i> is I really, truly,
 over the course of my life,</i>

00:20:40.571 --> 00:20:44.709
<i> would love to do
 for another person
 what you did for me.</i>

00:20:44.742 --> 00:20:47.012
<i> And I would love to be
 the kind of mom</i>

00:20:47.045 --> 00:20:49.014
<i> to somebody
 that you were to me.</i>

00:20:53.084 --> 00:20:55.754
And I would love to have
the kind of relationship

00:20:55.787 --> 00:20:58.723 align:start position:0%
as an adult
that you and I have now.

00:20:58.756 --> 00:21:01.726 align:start position:0%
And I just
wanted you to know that.

00:21:01.759 --> 00:21:03.728 align:start size:66% position:34%
